<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dorgon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1612">1612</a>–<a href="/years/1650">1650</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Prince Rui</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dorgon was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> prince, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nurhaci">Nurhaci</a>, who served as regent for the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shunzhi-emperor">Shunzhi Emperor</a> from 1643 to 1650. In 1644 he allied with the Ming general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-sangui">Wu Sangui</a>, defeated the rebel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-zicheng">Li Zicheng</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanhai-pass">Shanhai Pass</a> and occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, making it the Qing capital. He imposed the queue hairstyle on Han men and directed the conquest of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> until his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dorgon.jpg" alt="Dorgon" width="320" height="438" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dorgon,_the_Prince_Rui_(17th_century).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under the regent <b>Dorgon</b>, the Qing exploited this opening with great speed, capturing Beijing, enthroning the young Shunzhi Emperor as ruler of China and launching decades of campaigns against Ming loyalist resistance in the south.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The emperors watched their kinsmen closely: the regent <b>Dorgon</b> had ruled at the expense of rival princes, many of whom he demoted or imprisoned.</blockquote><a href="/societies/qing-china">Social Hierarchy of Qing China</a></li><li><blockquote>At the eastern end of the Great Wall the Qing prince-regent <b>Dorgon</b>, allied with the former Ming general Wu Sangui, defeated Li Zicheng, founder of the Shun dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-27">27 May in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hetu-ala">Hetu Ala City</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in Chengde, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.7,124.86&amp;year=1636" aria-label="Hetu Ala City on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7591 678 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Dorgon"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8041 948h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8041 948h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.7,124.86&amp;year=1636">What was here in 1636? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
